# CIVIL_03_SPECIFIC_PERFORMANCE_2026
# (Synthetic – for demo of Fact-Fit Gate and contradiction analysis)

## PW-1 (Plaintiff – Ramesh Kumar Sharma) – Key Cross points already covered in chief; defence cross focused on:

Defence Counsel: You never had the balance amount ready on 15.07.2024?
PW-1: I had the Demand Draft prepared on 10.07.2024. The original DD is on record.

Defence Counsel: Did you ever refuse to accept the title documents?
PW-1: No. I only asked for clarification on one mutation entry. I never refused to perform.

Defence Counsel: Why did you not file the suit immediately after 15.07.2024?
PW-1: I issued a second legal notice on 20.07.2024 and waited for response. The subsequent sale came to my knowledge only in October 2024.

## DW-1 (Defendant No. 1 – Suresh Chand Agarwal) – Key Contradictions Extracted

Q: When did you first decide not to execute the sale deed in favour of the Plaintiff?
A: (After hesitation) Around July 2024 because the Plaintiff was raising objections.

Q: Did you ever issue any written notice to the Plaintiff after 15.07.2024 stating that the contract stood cancelled?
A: No written notice was issued.

Q: Did you inform Defendant No. 2 about the existence of the Agreement dated 15.01.2024 before executing the sale deed on 12.09.2024?
A: I do not remember exactly… I think he knew there was some earlier talk.

Q: Is it correct that the entire sale consideration from Defendant No. 2 was received by you after 12.09.2024?
A: Yes, mostly after registration.

Q: You admit that the Plaintiff’s cheque of ₹15,00,000 was cleared and never returned?
A: Yes, it was cleared. I have not returned it.

## Investigating / Independent Witness (Sub-Registrar Clerk / Neighbour – if examined)

Key admission: Plaintiff was present on 15.07.2024 with documents; Defendant No. 1 did not appear.

## Fact-Fit Notes for Cross
- DW-1’s statement that “Plaintiff was raising objections” is unsupported by any contemporaneous letter.
- Admission that no cancellation notice was issued weakens the “time is essence + default” defence.
- Vague answer on notice to subsequent purchaser supports Plaintiff’s case under Section 19 SRA.
